<h2>Insurance company adjuster vs. public adjuster</h2>
<p>Most people meet an insurance adjuster for the first time after a loss, and that adjuster works for the insurance company. A public adjuster is licensed to represent the policyholder on the same claim. The difference looks like this:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Insurance company adjuster</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Represents</td><td>The insurer</td><td>The policyholder only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paid by</td><td>The insurance company (staff or independent firm)</td><td>The policyholder, under a written contract</td></tr>
<tr><td>Main job</td><td>Evaluate the claim under the insurer's guidelines</td><td>Document and present the full covered loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reading the policy</td><td>Applies the insurer's interpretation</td><td>Looks for every coverage that may apply</td></tr>
<tr><td>Workload after a storm</td><td>Often a large stack of claims at once</td><td>Your property, your policy, your file</td></tr>
<tr><td>Texas license</td><td>Adjuster license</td><td>Public adjuster license under Insurance Code Chapter 4102</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For more on how this plays out after a storm, read <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims-in-texas/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims in Texas</a> or <a href="/what-is-a-public-claims-adjuster/">what is a public claims adjuster?</a></p>

<h2>How Texas regulates public adjusters</h2>
<p>Public adjusting is a licensed, regulated profession in Texas. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> oversees public adjusters under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. The rules that matter most to you:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written contract.</strong> Representation begins only with a written contract on TDI-approved terms. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</li>
<li><strong>72-hour rescission.</strong> You may cancel within 72 hours of signing.</li>
<li><strong>"We represent the insured only."</strong> Every Texas public adjuster contract must state it.</li>
<li><strong>No repair conflicts.</strong> A public adjuster may not take part in repairing or rebuilding a property on a claim they adjust.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before you sign with anyone, look up their license on the TDI website. We encourage it.</p>

<h2>Our six-step claim process</h2>
<p>We do not use claim templates or shortcuts. Each property, policy, and loss is handled on its own facts, but the work generally follows six steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Initial consultation.</strong> We talk through what happened, where the claim stands, and whether a public adjuster is likely to help. This is the <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Property inspection.</strong> A thorough look at visible and hidden damage: roof, exterior, interior, and building systems. Documentation often includes Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Policy analysis.</strong> We read your declarations page, coverages, endorsements, deductibles (including any separate wind/hail deductible), and exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Claim preparation.</strong> A detailed estimate and supporting file, organized so the insurer can follow it line by line.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation.</strong> We communicate with the insurer, answer its requests, and pursue <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> when the facts support it.</li>
<li><strong>Resolution.</strong> We stay with the claim until it is resolved, including follow-up on recoverable depreciation where the policy provides it.</li>
</ol>
<p>You will know who is handling your claim and what comes next at every step.</p>

<h2>Clear communication</h2>
<p>The complaint we hear most from Dallas policyholders is simple: "I don't know where my claim stands." We make communication part of the job:</p>
<ul>
<li>An honest assessment of the claim's strengths and weak spots</li>
<li>Regular updates as the claim moves</li>
<li>Plain explanations of timelines and next steps</li>
<li>Straight answers, even when they are not the ones you hoped for</li>
</ul>
<p>Sometimes the honest answer is that you do not need us. If the insurer's offer looks fair, or the damage is small next to your deductible, we will say so.</p>

<h2>Ethics: what we will and will not do</h2>
<p>We never:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inflate damage or add items that are not part of the loss</li>
<li>Misrepresent facts to an insurer</li>
<li>Promise a specific settlement or outcome</li>
<li>Pressure anyone into signing</li>
</ul>
<p>We do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present legitimate claims thoroughly and accurately</li>
<li>Answer unsupported denials with documentation</li>
<li>Hold the claim to the terms of your policy</li>
</ul>
<p>We are also clear about our limits. <strong>We are not attorneys</strong>: we do not give legal advice or handle lawsuits, and if your situation calls for a lawyer we will tell you to consult a Texas attorney. <strong>We are not contractors</strong>: you keep full control over who repairs your property.</p>

<h2>When to call a Dallas public adjuster</h2>
<p>Some people call after they have already accepted a settlement based on an incomplete estimate. Others call early and avoid the common mistakes. It is worth reaching out if:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your claim was <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied</a> or <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid</a></li>
<li>The insurer keeps <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delaying</a> or reassigning adjusters</li>
<li>You are being asked for extensive documentation and are not sure what to send</li>
<li>The loss is large, complicated, or involves hidden damage</li>
<li>You want an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">independent review of the insurer's estimate</a> before you accept it</li>
</ul>
<p>Help is available at most stages of a claim, though your policy's deadlines still apply.</p>

<h2>Our commitment to Dallas</h2>
<p>North Texas weather keeps claims coming. Dallas County hail peaks in April and May, with a season that runs from March through June and a smaller bump in October. The county has seen grapefruit-size hail in Irving (May 2011), the EF4 Garland/Rowlett tornado (December 2015), an EF3 tornado through north Dallas (October 2019), Winter Storm Uri's burst pipes (February 2021), the August 2022 flash flood, and the May 28, 2024 windstorm that the National Weather Service found reached up to 95 mph in Garland. Our <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas storm history</a> page covers the NOAA record in more detail.</p>
